# next-auth@5 beta declares an OPTIONAL peer of @simplewebauthn@^9, but we intentionally
# run @simplewebauthn@13 (#629 — code adapted to v13 + tested). Without this, Vercel's fresh
# `npm install` fails with ERESOLVE and the deploy errors (local `npm ci` replays the lockfile
# and never re-resolves, which is why the gates missed it — #654). legacy-peer-deps restores
# npm-v6 behaviour: ignore the optional-peer version conflict. The lockfile already pins v13.
#
# AUDIT [9]/[12] — why some package.json entries are EXACT pins, not carets:
# Vercel's fresh `npm install` re-resolves ranges (see #654 above), so a caret is a live
# constraint in prod, not just lockfile decoration. Exact-pinned on purpose:
#   - next-auth 5.0.0-beta.31 — auth gates every session/admin route, and
#     `^5.0.0-beta.31` means `>=beta.31 <6.0.0`, i.e. ANY future beta (which have
#     shipped breaking changes between numbers) could land in a fresh install [9].
#     PINNED AT beta.31 (the version already running in prod), NOT bumped: moving the
#     auth library forward a beta is a behaviour change the pure-logic unit suite can't
#     validate. Bump it deliberately via renovate with a real deploy test, never as a
#     side-effect of pinning.
#   - prisma / @prisma/client / @prisma/adapter-pg 7.8.0 — renovate.json groups the
#     three to move in lockstep, but carets let them drift apart BETWEEN renovate
#     runs (CLI had resolved to 7.9.0 against client 7.8.0) [12]. Bump all three
#     together (renovate's `prisma` group does exactly that).
legacy-peer-deps=true
